-efficacy endpoint, paired contrast, and aggregate that would use it;
-neither sequence is selected or averaged. The independent Dr.GRPO and
-GRPO seed-59 evaluations remain valid. The core replay comparison has
-74 seed pairs: 14 five-seed model--domain blocks and Falcon3-1B
-\texttt{Countdown} at $n=4$, using seeds 55--58. The four-seed block is
-descriptive and has no confidence interval.
-
-Evaluation inputs are identified by source paths, job identities, and file
-hashes. Identical duplicate records count once. Conflicting repeated
-checkpoints are usable only when the sequence rules in
-Apps.~\ref{app:initial-provenance}--\ref{app:scheduled-provenance}
-identify a unique evaluation block; unresolved checkpoints remain missing.
-A missing initial or intermediate checkpoint does not invalidate an
-independently valid terminal endpoint. Source files outside the specified
-run and continuation records are excluded, and evaluation values do not
-select among attempts.
-
-Each training curve specifies its fixed seed cohort. Core objective pairs
-use their common terminal seed set and show a checkpoint only when both
-arms have observations there. Within an arm, the point averages the cohort
-seeds observed at that checkpoint; a ring marks a count below the full
-cohort size. Thus paired curves can use different observed seed subsets at
-an incomplete checkpoint, even though their fixed cohorts coincide.
-Their bands show observed seed ranges, not confidence intervals.
-A checkpoint with no observations remains a gap, and lines do not connect
-across missing scheduled steps. Accuracy and mode-count views use the
-same populations and gap rules. Supporting methods identify separate
-cohorts and, where applicable, individual unpaired histories. Numerical
-records give the exact seed sets, exclusions, per-checkpoint counts,
-source hashes, and evaluation rules.
-
-\subsection{Initial checkpoints and paired contrasts}
 \label{app:initial-provenance}
-
-A restarted attempt can evaluate the initial model again and append
-another step-0 block. The initial-checkpoint rule considers file order,
-step numbers, draw indices, and subsequent training evaluations. Files
-without a later training-step evaluation are excluded. Within a file,
-a step-0 block is continued when the next evaluation at a different step
-has a positive step number. Exactly one continued block must exist across
-the specified source files, and it must contain the four draw indices
-$0,1,2,3$. Metric values and effect estimates do not enter this selection.
-The rule identifies usable initial evaluations for 27 of 36 cells with
-conflicting step-0 records; nine remain ambiguous.
-
-Initial-to-terminal contrasts pair each method with its own initial
-evaluation. Terminal replay effects compare two trained checkpoints and
-require no initial evaluation.
-
-The initial-block sensitivity analysis contains nine model--domain--method
-contrasts. Excluding all conflicting initial blocks gives one to four
-defined seeds per contrast; the unique-block rule gives five in each.
-For example, Falcon3-1B MaxRL has three defined seeds in \texttt{Graph} and one
-in \texttt{PantryPlan} under the exclusion rule, and five under the unique-block
-rule. The mean signs agree under both rules; nominal intervals are shown
-only for the complete five-seed contrasts. At Falcon3-1B, MaxRL and
-Re:Max increase conditional diversity on \texttt{Graph}, with intervals excluding
-zero, while MaxRL concentrates on \texttt{Countdown}, \texttt{MathIR}, and \texttt{PantryPlan}.
-This heterogeneous result does not support extending the concentration
-claim in Section~\ref{sec:results-collapse} to every objective and domain.
-
-\subsection{Repeated scheduled checkpoints}
 \label{app:scheduled-provenance}
 
-For a repeated evaluation at scheduled step $S$, the sequence rule
-considers only source files containing an evaluation later than $S$.
-Rows at $S$ form draw blocks separated by deterministic trace rows or
-changes in step number. A block is continued when its next evaluation at
-a different step is later than $S$. The checkpoint is usable only if
-exactly one complete continued block with draw indices $0,1,2,3$ exists
-across the specified files. The rule uses ordering and checkpoint
-identities, rather than metric values, and includes the 27 initial
-checkpoints described above.
-
-Among 70 conflicting noninitial scheduled checkpoints, 27 have a unique
-complete continued block. The other 43 have multiple continued blocks
-and remain unavailable. A curve can still show the other seeds observed
-at such a step under the partial-cohort rule above; the unavailable
-seed's value is neither interpolated nor carried forward. The model repository supplies terminal inference weights, not the intermediate
-weights needed to re-evaluate these ambiguous checkpoints. Recorded
-intermediate evaluations therefore determine the available trajectory.
-
